## Budgets are inputs, not knowledge

There is no correct triangle count for a prop. Budgets come from the
project — from the target hardware, the frame time, and how much of the
screen the asset occupies — and they have moved by two orders of
magnitude across hardware generations. Any per-platform table you can
recall is folklore.

So: the budget is whatever the parameters say. If none was given, ASK.
Do not supply one from memory and do not treat a number you invented as
a constraint you met.

## What the export gate checks

- Transforms applied. Non-uniform object scale is hostile to engines,
  several of which accept only uniform scale on renderable geometry.
- Axis and unit convention stated per FORMAT, not per engine. The
  correct forward and up axes differ between formats for the same
  target, so a single remembered rule is wrong somewhere.
- Round trip verified. Export, re-import, re-measure. An export you did
  not read back is a claim, not a result.

## Texturing, when there is any

Hold WORLD-SPACE texel density consistent across the asset — the same
texture area per unit of surface, so one side of a box does not read
softer than another. Screen-space density is a different quantity and
necessarily varies with distance; do not conflate them.

Seams: hide them where they will not be seen, and understand what you
are trading. Fewer seams means more distortion — that is a measured
trade-off, not a free win. Say which side of it you chose.

Report every number you produce. Triangle count, material count, texture
sizes, the budget you were given and whether you met it.
